{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI Ethics and Ethobot 3.2","title":"Moving From Debate to Policy","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting moving from debate to policy to school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"06","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2","cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/04/05"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Moving From Debate to Policy\n\n![Moving From Debate to Policy textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/04-06-moving-from-debate-to-policy.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA good classroom debate generates heat, conviction, and a dozen competing positions. None of that helps you on Monday morning when a student actually turns in an AI-assisted essay and asks whether it counts. Debate clarifies values; it does not produce a rule you can apply consistently to thirty students. This section is about the translation step, converting the energy and the disagreements of a debate into a policy that is observable, enforceable, and fair, which is a genuinely different skill from arguing well. Many teachers are strong at the first and surprisingly weak at the second.\n\nThe translation is hard because it forces choices a debate lets you dodge. A debate can hold \"AI helps stuck students\" and \"AI is unfair if only some use it\" in tension forever; a policy has to do something about both at once. Worse, a policy has to confront enforcement reality (you usually cannot reliably detect AI use), which means the rule you can actually run may differ from the rule you wish you could. Naming that gap honestly, and designing around it, is what makes a policy defensible rather than aspirational.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Extract the shared value beneath competing debate positions rather than picking the loudest claim.\n- Rewrite a vague principle (\"use AI responsibly\") as an observable rule a colleague could apply the same way.\n- Match the enforcement mechanism to reality: base a rule on disclosure when detection is unreliable.\n- Build in an equity exception so the rule does not silently penalize students with less access.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA class debates \"Should students be allowed to use AI on take-home essays?\" and produces three sharp claims: AI helps students who get stuck, it is unfair if only some students use it, and you cannot tell who used it anyway. The teacher could write \"Our policy is to use AI ethically and responsibly,\" which sounds finished and decides nothing. Or she could treat the three claims as raw material: a shared value (fairness of access and transparency), an enforcement fact (detection is unreliable), and a constraint (some students lack home internet). The second path produces a rule she can apply to an actual essay; the first produces a sentence she will reinterpret differently for every student who tests it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nMoving from debate to policy is a four-move translation, and skipping any move leaves you with a slogan instead of a rule.\n\nThe first move is to extract the shared value, not the dominant voice. Beneath \"AI helps stuck students\" and \"it's unfair if only some use it\" sits a common concern with fairness of access. A policy built on the shared value resolves the disagreement; a policy built on the loudest claim merely takes a side.\n\nThe second move is to make the rule observable. \"Use AI responsibly\" cannot be applied consistently because two teachers will read \"responsibly\" differently. \"You may use AI to brainstorm and to check grammar, but not to generate submitted paragraphs, and you must cite any AI use in a footnote\" can be checked against an actual essay.\n\nThe third move is to match enforcement to reality. If detection is unreliable, a rule that depends on catching violators is theater. The honest design bases compliance on disclosure rather than surveillance, which is both more enforceable and more respectful.\n\nThe fourth move is to write an equity exception. A rule that assumes home internet quietly punishes students who lack it; providing class-time access keeps the rule from converting a resource gap into a discipline problem.\n\nThe course anchors steer the translation. **Teacher professional judgment** is what extracts the shared value and decides what \"observable\" means in your room. **Critical edtech studies** is what catches the hidden equity penalty before it becomes policy. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** informs the enforcement-reality move, since \"AI detectors\" are themselves unreliable, often biased tools whose error rates fall hardest on non-native writers: across seven widely used GPT detectors, essays by non-native English writers were falsely flagged as AI-generated 61 percent of the time on average, while the same detectors were near-perfect on native-speaker essays, and the bias tracked linguistic features rather than actual AI use (Liang et al., 2023).\n\n## Worked Example: Turning a Class Debate Into an Enforceable Rule\n\nA class debated \"Should students be allowed to use AI on take-home essays?\" The debate produced strong opinions but no rule a teacher could apply on Monday. This section converts debate into policy. Work it as numbered steps.\n\nWhat the debate produced:\n\n1. \"AI helps students who get stuck.\"\n2. \"It is unfair if some students use it and some do not.\"\n3. \"You cannot tell who used it anyway.\"\n\nStep-by-step debate-to-policy conversion:\n\n- Step 1. Extract the shared value, not the loudest claim. The points converge on fairness of access and transparency.\n- Step 2. Write a rule that is observable. Vague: \"Use AI responsibly.\" Observable: \"You may use AI to brainstorm and check grammar, but not to generate full paragraphs; cite any AI use in a footnote.\"\n- Step 3. Name the enforcement reality. Since detection is unreliable (point 3), base the rule on disclosure, not surveillance.\n- Step 4. State the exception path. A student without home internet may use class time, so the rule does not punish lack of access.\n\nBefore (weak): \"Our policy is to use AI ethically and responsibly.\" This is unenforceable and hides the disagreements.\n\nAfter (strong): \"Policy: AI permitted for brainstorming and grammar, not for generating submitted text; all AI use disclosed in a footnote; enforced by disclosure not detection because detection is unreliable; class-time access provided so the rule does not penalize students without home internet. This resolves the fairness and transparency tensions the debate raised.\"\n\nThe strong policy is observable, names how it is enforced, and includes an equity exception, which a colleague could apply consistently.\n\nPut the four translation moves in the order that takes a debate to an enforceable rule.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Extract the shared value beneath the competing claims, not the loudest voice\", \"Rewrite the rule so it is observable: two teachers reach the same call on the same essay\", \"Match enforcement to reality: lean on disclosure where detection is unreliable\", \"Write an equity exception so the rule does not punish a resource gap\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore a policy leaves your desk, run the four-move test:\n\n1. **Shared value:** Does the rule rest on the value beneath the debate, not on whoever argued hardest?\n2. **Observable:** Could two teachers apply this rule to the same essay and reach the same call?\n3. **Enforceable as written:** Does the mechanism match reality, leaning on disclosure where detection fails?\n4. **Equity exception:** Does the rule avoid punishing students for a resource gap they did not choose?\n\nA policy that fails the observable test is a slogan; one that fails the equity test will quietly fall hardest on the students with the least.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher writes: 'Students must use AI responsibly and with integrity.' Why does this fail the observable test even though it sounds principled?\" explanation=\"Two teachers will read 'responsibly' and 'integrity' differently, so the same essay could pass for one and fail for another. An observable rule names what is allowed (brainstorm, grammar) and what is not (generating submitted paragraphs) so any colleague reaches the same call.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It does not fail; integrity is a clear standard\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Two teachers could apply it to the same essay and reach opposite calls\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is too short to be a real policy\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It mentions AI but not the vendor name\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nNow it is Monday. A real essay forces the enforcement choice the debate let everyone dodge.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Monday: enforcing the AI-essay rule\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your draft policy says AI may be used to brainstorm and check grammar but not to generate submitted paragraphs. A colleague proposes enforcing it with an AI-detector tool. Detection of AI text is unreliable. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt the AI detector; catching violators deters everyone\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"A rule that depends on unreliable detection is theater, and detector false positives land disproportionately on English learners, producing the unfairness the policy meant to prevent.\"},{\"label\":\"Base compliance on a required footnote disclosing any AI use\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Disclosure matches the enforcement reality: it is more enforceable than unreliable detection and more respectful of students.\"},{\"label\":\"Drop the rule because you cannot prove violations\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Abandoning the rule throws away the shared value (fairness and transparency). The fix is to change the mechanism, not the goal.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"A flagged essay belongs to an English learner whose phrasing the detector misread. What harm did the detector just cause?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It converted a resource and language difference into an integrity charge\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Switch to disclosure-based enforcement.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"A student says they have no home internet and could not use AI to brainstorm like classmates. Your observable rule otherwise stands. What do you add?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; the rule applies equally to everyone\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Formal equality here hides a resource gap: the rule quietly penalizes the student for something they did not choose.\"},{\"label\":\"An equity exception: class-time access so the rule does not punish lack of home internet\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"This keeps the rule from turning a resource gap into a discipline problem, which is the fourth move.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what should you change, the goal or the mechanism?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The mechanism: enforce by disclosure, not detection\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Keep the shared value; fix how it is enforced.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"Re-examine: does ’equal treatment’ produce a fair outcome when access differs?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; add a class-time access exception\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Equity sometimes requires an unequal accommodation to reach a fair result.\"}],\"feedback\":\"\"},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Your policy enforces by disclosure and includes a class-time access exception.\",\"feedback\":\"A defensible policy matches enforcement to reality and adds an equity exception, so it does not fall hardest on the students with the least.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is writing a rule that sounds principled but cannot be applied: \"act with integrity\" passes no observable test and so means whatever each teacher decides in the moment. The second is reaching for surveillance to enforce what disclosure should handle, trusting an AI detector whose false positives land disproportionately on English learners and produce exactly the unfairness the policy was meant to prevent.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four moves to a different debate: whether students may use AI image generators for a history poster project. The debate splits over creativity (\"it lets weak artists shine\") and authenticity (\"it isn't their work\"). Extract the shared value: the assignment is about historical understanding, not drawing skill. Make it observable: \"AI-generated images are allowed if every image is captioned with the prompt used and a one-sentence historical justification.\" Match enforcement to reality: you cannot reliably tell AI art from stock art, so require the prompt disclosure rather than policing the image source. Write the equity exception: students without reliable home devices may generate images on classroom machines during a set period. The result is a rule a substitute teacher could apply to any poster in the stack, which a debate, however lively, never produces on its own.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nKeep a two-column note: each debate claim on the left, what it contributes to the policy on the right (a shared value, an enforcement fact, a constraint). A strong reader can show the path from three conflicting opinions to one applicable rule, and can point to the line that handles the enforcement gap and the line that protects access. If your policy reads like a value statement rather than a procedure, you have summarized the debate instead of resolving it.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong policy memo names the shared value, states an observable rule, declares the enforcement mechanism honestly, and includes an equity exception. Tone and formatting are the last layer. If the rule is not observable or has no equity exception, mark the gap and ask BigAL to test your rule against two borderline student cases rather than for a general explanation of policy.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like this: \"I based enforcement on footnoted disclosure rather than AI detection because detectors misfire on English learners, and I added class-time access so the rule does not turn a home-internet gap into an academic-integrity charge.\"\n\n**Legal floor.** The memo's privacy and consent checks are not free-floating values; they rest on statute. FERPA (34 CFR Part 99) generally prohibits disclosing personally identifiable information from student education records without prior parental or eligible-student consent, and COPPA governs data collected from students under 13. The FTC's amended COPPA Rule (effective June 23, 2025, with full compliance by April 22, 2026) expands \"personal information\" to include biometric and government-issued identifiers, requires separate verifiable parental consent for third-party disclosures, and adds requirements bearing on using children's data to train AI. A policy memo whose privacy clause cannot point at this floor is an aspiration, not a check.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why basing enforcement on disclosure rather than detection is both more enforceable and more respectful, given that AI detectors are unreliable.\" answer=\"Detection cannot reliably tell AI-written text from human text, so a rule that depends on catching violators will miss real cases and flag innocent ones, and its false positives fall hardest on non-native writers whose phrasing the detector misreads. That makes detection both ineffective (it does not enforce the rule consistently) and unfair (it manufactures the very inequity the policy was meant to prevent). Disclosure asks students to footnote any AI use, which is something a teacher can actually check and which treats students as accountable participants rather than suspects. It is more enforceable because the mechanism matches reality, and more respectful because it does not subject students to a faulty surveillance tool.\"></self-explain>\n\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the policy from a single classroom to a whole department, where you no longer control how each colleague interprets \"observable.\" A rule clear enough for you may still drift across five teachers' judgment. Working this version tests whether your rule is truly observable or only observable-to-you, and it usually forces a worked example or two pasted right into the policy so colleagues converge on the same calls.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/04-06-moving-from-debate-to-policy-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"04.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks for CAT 531 04.06. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\nThese are the concepts this section connects. Trace how a debate becomes an observable, enforceable rule through evidence-based revision.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_ethics_and_ethobot_3_2\", \"cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Selwyn, N. (2022). Education and technology: Key issues and debates (3rd ed.). Bloomsbury Academic. ISBN 9781350145559. https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/education-and-technology-9781350145559/\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. *Learning, Media and Technology, 45*(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n- Liang, W., Yuksekgonul, M., Mao, Y., Wu, E., & Zou, J. (2023). GPT detectors are biased against non-native English writers. *Patterns, 4*(7), 100779. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779\n- U.S. Department of Education. Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), 34 CFR Part 99 (20 U.S.C. § 1232g). https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/ferpa\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025). Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule, Final Amendments. *Federal Register* (Apr. 22, 2025). https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","stem":"After a class debate, a memo states the new rule as 'Students must use AI responsibly and ethically.' Why is this not yet a usable policy?","options":["It is too short for a formal memo.","It names no observable behavior, so a teacher cannot tell on Monday what is permitted and what is not.","It does not mention the textbook.","It uses two adjectives instead of one."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A policy must convert values into observable rules a teacher can apply consistently.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_vague_values_as_policy","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","stem":"A student in the debate noted 'you cannot really tell who used AI anyway.' How should that point shape the written policy?","options":["Ignore it, since rules should assume detection works.","Base enforcement on required disclosure rather than detection, because detection is unreliable.","Ban all take-home essays to remove the problem.","Add a stricter punishment to deter violations."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"When detection is unreliable, a disclosure-based rule is both enforceable and fairer than surveillance.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_enforcement_by_detection","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","stem":"The debate raised that some students lack home internet. Which policy element addresses that without abandoning the rule?","options":["Require all work to be done at home regardless.","Provide class time or school devices so the rule does not penalize students for unequal access.","Exempt those students from the assignment entirely.","Remove the disclosure requirement for everyone."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An equity exception keeps the rule fair by not punishing a circumstance rather than a choice.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_policy_ignores_access","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_moving_from_debate_to_policy_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which final policy best converts the debate's fairness and transparency concerns into an applicable rule?","options":["AI use is encouraged when appropriate.","AI permitted for brainstorming and grammar but not for generating submitted text; all use disclosed in a footnote; enforced by disclosure; class time provided for students without home access.","Teachers will decide case by case without a written rule.","AI is banned and any suspected use results in a zero."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strong policy is observable, states enforcement, and includes an equity exception, so it is auditable and applicable.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_4_6_vague_values_as_policy","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","cat531_supplement_moving_from_debate_to_policy"]}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"a971f85c290c9a72ada38e8760cb80c45b76418e6da73527378c28c4e6db62bc","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}